  "response": "# Verdict: REJECT\n\n**Overall Assessment**: The component fails core accessibility requirements for form controls and error handling. It lacks a programmatic label, does not associate error messages with the input field, and fails to communicate invalid state to assistive technology. While the `accept` attribute is present, the interaction model for screen reader users is broken.\n\n**Pre-commitment Predictions**:\n1.  **Missing Label:** The \"Upload file\" text is a plain `<div>`, not a `<label>`. Axe-core confirms this with a critical `label` violation.\n2.  **Error Association Gap:** The error message renders visually but lacks an `id` and `aria-describedby` association, meaning screen reader users won't know which field failed validation.\n3.  **Missing Invalid State:** The input does not update `aria-invalid` when the `error` state is active, leaving the invalid state invisible to assistive technology.\n\n**Critical Findings** (blocks access):\n\n1.  **File Input Lacks a Programmatic Label**\n    -   **Evidence**: `BuggyFileInput.jsx:18` renders `<div>Upload file</div>` instead of a `<label>`. Axe-core reports a critical `label` violation on the input element.\n    -   **User Group Impacted**: Screen reader users (cannot identify the purpose of the control).\n    -   **WCAG/APG Citation**: WCAG 1.3.1 Info and Relationships; WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value.\n    -   **Fix**: Replace the `<div>` with a native `<label htmlFor=\"file-upload\">Upload file</label>` and add `id=\"file-upload\"` to the input.\n\n2.  **Error Message Not Associated with Input**\n    -   **Evidence**: `BuggyFileInput.jsx:25` renders the error in a `<div className=\"error-message\">`. The input (line 19) has no `aria-describedby` attribute pointing to this div's ID. Screen reader census shows \"Upload file\" and \"File too large\" as separate, unconnected phrases.\n    -   **User Group Impacted**: Screen reader users (cannot determine which field the error applies to).\n    -   **WCAG/APG Citation**: WCAG 3.3.1 Error Identification; WAI-ARIA Form pattern.\n    -   **Fix**: Add `id=\"file-error\"` to the error `<div>` and `aria-describedby=\"file-error\"` to the input.\n\n**Major Findings** (significantly degrades experience):\n\n1.  **Invalid State Not Communicated Programmatically**\n    -   **Evidence**: `BuggyFileInput.jsx:15-23` toggles the `error` state but does not update the input's `aria-invalid` attribute. Screen reader users will hear \"Choose File\" (UA name) regardless of whether validation failed.\n    -   **User Group Impacted**: Screen reader users (cannot distinguish between valid and invalid states without tabbing to the field).\n    -   **WCAG/APG Citation**: WCAG 3.3.1 Error Identification; WAI-ARIA APG Form pattern.\n    -   **Fix**: Add `aria-invalid={!!error}` to the `<input>` element.\n\n**Minor Findings** (friction but workaround exists):\n\n1.  **File Type Restrictions Not Visible to Sighted Users**\n    -   **Evidence**: The `accept=\".pdf,.doc,.docx\"` attribute is present on the input, but there is no visible text explaining this restriction. Sighted users may attempt to upload unsupported files and only discover the limitation after validation.\n    -   **Fix**: Add a visible help text element (e.g., `<span>Accepted formats: PDF, DOC, DOCX</span>`) and associate it with the input via `aria-describedby`.\n\n**What's Missing** (gaps, unhandled edge cases):\n\n-   **Missing Label Element**: The component relies on the browser's native \"Choose File\" label because no accessible name is provided.\n-   **Missing Error Association**: No `id`/`aria-describedby` pairing between the error message and the input field.\n-   **Missing Invalid State**: No `aria-invalid` attribute toggling on the input element.\n\n**Multi-Perspective Notes**:\n\n-   **Screen reader user**: Hears \"Choose File\" (UA name) or nothing if UA names are stripped. The text \"Upload file\" is read as a separate phrase, disconnected from the input. When an error occurs, they hear \"File too large\" but don't know which field it belongs to unless they tab back to it.\n-   **Keyboard-only user**: Can tab to the input and activate it (opens file dialog). No keyboard traps, but no visual or programmatic guidance on allowed file types.\n-   **Low vision user**: The \"Upload file\" text is a plain `<div>`, which may not be styled as a label, potentially causing confusion about its purpose.\n-   **Cognitive accessibility**: The error message appears visually but isn't linked to the field programmatically. If this component were part of a larger form, users might struggle to locate the specific field that failed validation.\n\n**Verdict Justification**: The component fails to meet fundamental requirements for form labeling and error association. Screen reader users cannot identify the input's purpose or understand which field has an error. These are not minor design gaps; they are functional barriers for assistive technology users.\n\n**Open Questions (unscored)**:\n-   Does the parent application provide a global skip link? (Not visible in this snippet).\n-   Is the `accept` attribute sufficient for all target browsers, or should a custom file picker be considered for better control over validation messaging?",
